# Project Rules - Slack Standup Bot

## 1. Project Architecture

### 1.1 Modular Structure
The application follows a modular architecture with clear separation of concerns:

```
/slack-standup-bot
├── src/
│   ├── app.js                    # Entry point and server configuration
│   ├── routes/                   # API route handlers
│   │   ├── commands.js           # Slash command handling
│   │   ├── events.js             # Slack events handling
│   │   ├── interact.js           # Interactive component handling
│   │   └── auth.js               # OAuth and authentication
│   ├── services/                 # Business logic
│   │   ├── standupScheduler.js   # Scheduling functionality
│   │   ├── standupLogic.js       # Core standup functionality
│   │   ├── teamStore.js          # Team/user data management
│   │   └── slackClient.js        # Slack API client wrapper
│   └── configStore.js            # Configuration storage
├── .env                          # Environment variables
└── package.json                  # Dependencies and scripts
```

### 1.2 Dependency Principles
- Keep external dependencies to a minimum
- Use specific versions in package.json to ensure reproducible builds
- Prefer native Node.js modules when possible
- All API interactions with Slack should go through the slackClient service

## 2. Code Organization

### 2.1 File Structure
- One export per file (prefer module pattern)
- Group related functionality in appropriate directories
- Use descriptive filenames that indicate functionality
- Keep files under 200 lines - split if larger

### 2.2 Naming Conventions
- Use camelCase for variables, functions, and methods
- Use PascalCase for classes
- Use kebab-case for file names
- Use UPPER_SNAKE_CASE for constants and environment variables

## 3. Feature Development Workflow

### 3.1 Adding New Features
1. Update the configuration schema if needed
2. Add necessary service functions
3. Connect to the appropriate route handler
4. Add any new Slack API scopes to documentation
5. Update tests

### 3.2 Feature Approval Process
- Document the feature purpose and design
- Create a minimal viable implementation
- Include appropriate test coverage
- Submit for code review before merging

## 4. Configuration Management

### 4.1 Environment Variables
- Store all sensitive information in environment variables
- Include a .env.example file with all required variables (without values)
- Document all environment variables in README.md
- Use a default fallback for non-sensitive configuration

### 4.2 Application Configuration
- Store team-specific configuration in the JSON store
- Validate all configuration before use
- Provide clear error messages for misconfiguration

## 5. Deployment Guidelines

### 5.1 Deployment Process
- Use continuous deployment when possible
- Test in staging environment before production
- Maintain zero-downtime deployments
- Keep deployment documentation updated

### 5.2 Hosting Requirements
- Ensure hosting platform supports persistent storage
- Configure memory/CPU requirements based on team size
- Set up monitoring and alerting
- Implement self-ping to prevent sleeping on free tiers

## 6. Documentation Requirements

### 6.1 Code Documentation
- Document all functions with JSDoc comments
- Include @param and @return tags
- Explain complex logic with inline comments
- Update documentation when changing functionality

### 6.2 Project Documentation
- Maintain an up-to-date README.md with:
  - Project overview
  - Setup instructions
  - Configuration options
  - Troubleshooting guides
- Document Slack app configuration process with screenshots

## 7. Version Control

### 7.1 Git Workflow
- Use feature branches for development
- Use conventional commit messages
- Squash commits before merging to main
- Tag all releases with semantic versioning

### 7.2 Pull Request Guidelines
- Include a description of changes
- Reference related issues
- Ensure all tests pass
- Address all code review comments

## 8. Quality Assurance

### 8.1 Testing Requirements
- Maintain minimum 80% test coverage
- Include unit tests for all business logic
- Include integration tests for all routes
- Mock external dependencies

### 8.2 Code Quality
- Use ESLint with appropriate rule set
- Run automated code quality checks on CI
- Address all linting warnings before merging
- Perform regular dependency updates 